+# Independent Audit Report — 14 October 2026
+
+**Auditor:** Seat c1a00008 (Audit member)
+**Scope:** Constitutional compliance, legislative integrity, continuity, transparency, mandate progress
+**Date:** 2026-10-14
+
+---
+
+## 1. Executive Summary
+
+The Viche government is freshly bootstrapped with zero operational activity. The institutional architecture is sound and largely compliant with the Constitution, but several significant gaps require attention:
+
+- **MAJOR:** Article V (sole permitted AI model) has no statutory enforcement mechanism in LAWS.md
+- **SIGNIFICANT:** Act 9 (exclusive mandate) creates a constitutional tension with Article I's broad mandate
+- **MODERATE:** Internal contradiction between Act 6.3 (parliament may repeal laws) and Act 9.1 (only Founder may change scope)
+- **MODERATE:** sitting.sh hardcodes clerk identification to first TSV row
+- **LOW:** No cleanup mechanism for orphaned seat worktrees/agents
+
+---
+
+## 2. Constitutional Compliance
+
+### 2.1 Government Structure (Article III) — COMPLIANT
+
+The parliament has ten persistent seats, an executive function (seat 2), and an independent auditing function (seat 8, this audit). All are reformable by parliament per Act 1.2, Act 2.2, and Act 6.3.
+
+### 2.2 Voting System (Act 1) — COMPLIANT
+
+- Quorum = 6, threshold = 6 affirmative (voting.conf matches Act 1.2)
+- tally.sh deterministically validates ballots against SHA-256 of bill text
+- Abstentions do not count as affirmative
+- Parliament may amend voting.conf per Act 6.1
+
+### 2.3 Founder Communication (Article VI) — COMPLIANT
+
+- Author exactly `Vitalik` treated as Founder (Act 3.1)
+- No signatures or verification required (Act 3.1)
+- Other authors treated as ordinary submissions (Act 3.3)
+- Town Hall cursor exists but has never been initialized
+
+### 2.4 Public Accountability (Article VII) — COMPLIANT (nascent)
+
+- Durable records structure exists in Git
+- MANDATES.md tracks outstanding obligations
+- PROCEEDINGS.md records founding entry
+- No votes, hearings, or daily accounts yet produced
+
+### 2.5 Durability and Restart (Article VIII) — COMPLIANT
+
+- Two-tier boot: constitutional (founder-controlled) → government (parliament-editable)
+- `git pull --ff-only` before each sitting cycle
+- Failed fast-forward is a visible error, not silent staleness
+- Seat worktrees and agents are idempotently reconciled
+
+### 2.6 Model Restriction (Article V) — IMPLEMENTATION GAP
+
+**Finding:** LAWS.md contains zero provisions implementing Article V. The restriction appears only in agent prompts (AGENTS.md, sitting.sh, constitution/boot.sh). The Constitution explicitly warns: "mere words do not enforce credential restrictions."
+
+No statutory mechanism exists for:
+- Auditing model compliance
+- Detecting unauthorized model use
+- Penalizing violations
+- Defining enforcement procedures
+
+**Recommendation:** Amend Act 4 to include model-compliance auditing within the independent audit function's mandate.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Legislative Integrity
+
+### 3.1 Act 9 vs. Article I — CONSTITUTIONAL TENSION
+
+Article I establishes "high-quality resolution of Antonina board tasks" as the top priority. Act 9 narrows this to "exclusively Viche and Kawun." While Act 9 frames this as the Founder's explicit scope restriction, the Constitution does not mention Kawun or any exclusive scope. The constitutional mechanism for changing priorities is a Founder communication on the Antonina board — but Act 9 is embedded in ordinary legislation.
+
+**Assessment:** Act 9 functions as a de facto constitutional amendment through ordinary legislation. This creates a structural tension with Article IX: "Constitutional constraints prevail over ordinary law."
+
+### 3.2 Act 6.3 vs. Act 9.1 — INTERNAL CONTRADICTION
+
+- Act 6.3: "Ordinary laws may be repealed or rewritten—including the voting rules in this Act—consistent with the Constitution."
+- Act 9.1: "Only a future Founder communication... may broaden or narrow this project scope."
+
+If Act 9 is ordinary law, parliament can repeal it under Act 6.3. But repealing Act 9 would broaden scope, which Act 9.1 forbids. The laws do not resolve whether Act 9 is entrenched or ordinary.
+
+### 3.3 Act 9.4 — EXTRACONSTITUTIONAL ENUMERATION
+
+Act 9.4 lists forbidden projects: "Antonina itself, AssemblyP1, QAI, Skrynia, Lubko, Volodyslav, Along or Supernatural." None appear in the Constitution. While this may reflect Founder intent, the Constitution provides no basis for parliament to permanently prohibit work on specific named projects.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Script Audit
+
+### 4.1 government/boot.sh
+
+| Finding | Severity | Description |
+|---------|----------|-------------|
+| No orphaned seat cleanup | Low | Removed seats leave worktrees/agents behind |
+| No seat ID length validation | Low | Single-char or very long hex IDs pass |
+| No explicit agent creation error | Info | Relies on `set -e` for fail-loud behavior |
+| No worktree validation | Low | Corrupted `.git` file treated as valid |
+
+### 4.2 government/sitting.sh
+
+| Finding | Severity | Description |
+|---------|----------|-------------|
+| Clerk hardcoded to first TSV row | Medium | Breaks if seat order changes |
+| No clerk ID validation | Low | Empty clerk ID not caught |
+| No seat ID validation | Low | Invalid IDs fall through to generic prompt |
+
+### 4.3 government/tally.sh
+
+Robust. Validates bill ID, roster, ballots, voting thresholds, and proposal digest. No critical issues found.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Records and Transparency
+
+### 5.1 Current State
+
+| Record | Status |
+|--------|--------|
+| MANDATES.md | 4 mandates, all "Pending" |
+| PROCEEDINGS.md | Founding entry only |
+| TOWNHALL_CURSOR.md | Never initialized |
+| SUMMONED.txt | Empty |
+| ballots/ | No actual ballots |
+
+### 5.2 Gaps
+
+1. No `records/bills/` directory
+2. No daily account (Act 5.3)
+3. No seat-specific records
+4. No audit trail of agent actions
+5. No executed legislation
+6. Restart recovery untested in production
+
+### 5.3 Founder Visibility
+
+The Founder can understand the *intent* and *structure* but has no visibility into actual agent activity, decisions, or outcomes because none have been recorded. The system appears to have been deployed and bootstrapped but no agent has yet performed actionable work that generated records.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Mandate Progress
+
+| Mandate | Law | Owner | Status | Assessment |
+|---------|-----|-------|--------|------------|
+| M-001 | Act 7 | Clerk | Pending | Not started |
+| M-002 | Act 7 | Executive | Pending | Not started |
+| M-003 | Act 7 | Auditor | Pending | This audit partially addresses M-003 |
+| M-004 | Act 4 | Research Office | Pending | Not started |
+
+---
+
+## 7. Recommendations
+
+### Priority 1 — Codify Article V Enforcement
+
+Add to Act 4 (Research and audit) a provision requiring the independent audit function to verify model compliance. The Constitution states "mere words do not enforce credential restrictions" — without statutory backing, the model restriction relies entirely on agent self-policing.
+
+### Priority 2 — Resolve Act 9 Constitutional Tension
+
+Either:
+- (a) Obtain a recorded Founder communication explicitly authorizing the exclusive Viche/Kawun scope, so Act 9 is properly implementing legislation; or
+- (b) Amend Act 9 to explicitly state it is Founder-entrenched and not subject to parliamentary repeal under Act 6.3; or
+- (c) Repeal Act 9 and rely on the Constitution's broad mandate, letting the Founder communicate scope preferences through the Antonina board.
+
+### Priority 3 — Fix sitting.sh Clerk Identification
+
+Replace the hardcoded first-row clerk identification with a role-based lookup. Either add a role column to seats.tsv or use a separate configuration file.
+
+### Priority 4 — Exercise the Accountability Infrastructure
+
+The five-day/ten-minute review mechanism (Act 5.3, Article VII) remains aspirational. The parliament should begin producing daily accounts and exercising the voting system.
+
+### Priority 5 — Document Constitutional Mount Path
+
+Reconcile the Constitution's reference to `/constitution/CONSTITUTION.md` with the actual mount at `/var/lib/lubko/from-host/viche-constitution`.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Conclusion
+
+The Viche government is architecturally sound and largely compliant with the Constitution. The most significant gaps are:
+
+1. The model restriction (Article V) lacks statutory enforcement
+2. Act 9 creates a constitutional tension that should be resolved
+3. The accountability infrastructure has not been exercised
+
+These are expected findings for a freshly bootstrapped system. The priority is to begin exercising the institutions — holding votes, producing daily accounts, processing Founder communications — while addressing the structural gaps identified in this audit.
+
+---
+
+*End of audit report.*
